 is the reverse version of [`advance_by`]. This method will"},{"DocComment":" eagerly skip `n` elements starting from the back by calling [`next_back`] up"},{"DocComment":" to `n` times until [`None`] is encountered."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `advance_back_by(n)` will return `Ok(())` if the iterator successfully advances by"},{"DocComment":" `n` elements, or a `Err(NonZero<usize>)` with value `k` if [`None`] is encountered, where `k`"},{"DocComment":" is remaining number of steps that could not be advanced because the iterator ran out."},{"DocComment":" If `self` is empty and `n` is non-zero, then this returns `Err(n)`."},{"DocComment":" Otherwise, `k` is always less than `n`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Calling `advance_back_by(0)` can do meaningful work, for example [`Flatten`] can advance its"},{"DocComment":" outer iterator until it finds an inner iterator that is not empty, which then often"},{"DocComment":" allows it to return a more accurate `size_hint()` than in its initial state."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`advance_by`]: Iterator::advance_by"},{"DocComment":" [`Flatten`]: crate::iter::Flatten"},{"DocComment":" [`next_back`]: DoubleEndedIterator::next_back"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Basic usage:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" #![feature(iter_advance_by)]"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" use std::num::NonZero;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let a = [3, 4, 5, 6];"},{"DocComment":" let mut iter = a.iter();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.advance_back_by(2), Ok(()));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.next_back(), Some(&4));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.advance_back_by(0), Ok(()));"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(iter.advance_back_by(100), Err(NonZero::new(99).unwrap())); // only `&3` was skipped"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`Ok(())`]: Ok"},{"DocComment":" [`Err(k)`]: Err"}],"inline":"Hint","ren